  {
    "episode_id": 95657,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95657.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 129,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, and you know, Sammy, people forget very quickly, right? In 2018, we had incredible wins here in the state of Florida. We didn't win every race. Rick Scott won in 2018 barely with only 10,000 votes, but I won my congressional seat. This was a seat that had a very popular two-term Republican incumbent. I didn't come from politics before that I had been working at FIU at the medical school, but I was working to expand access to healthcare. And the Republican at the time was trying to take away the ACA. And so that's what prompted me to run. I wanted to stand up for my community. No one thought I could beat this guy. I did. I beat him by two points. We also won another congressional seat that year. We won statewide. Nikki Fried, who's now the chair of the party, she won her seat statewide agricultural commissioner, which is at a very, very important seat here in Florida and part of the overall cabinet. So let's not forget what just happened a few years ago, right? I mean, people seem to forget what is possible here in Florida. then we hit COVID. People were very much isolated. It was a very difficult and strange election in 2020. And in 2022, we had zero investments. That's what happens when no one invests in the state, when no one invests in communicating directly to voters and getting our voters to turn out. It was a very difficult year. DeSantis was still very popular because one of the things that he did is open up the economy very quickly. And that's what people wanted and needed here because our economy is based on tourism, right? And there was that need for us to get back on track. He did it sooner than most states. And that was popular for him at the time. So he was still enjoying that popularity in 2022. We had zero investments, no party infrastructure, no one came out to vote. And that's what happened. What's happening now is we have a very important amendment on the ballot to protect abortion rights. Florida passed the most extreme and an abortion with hardly any exceptions to rape and incest, which we're now living under. It was put into effect on May 1st, but we also have now the opportunity to come out and protect abortion rights, make sure that we enshrine those rights into the state constitution. But what I'm saying to everyone is, look, it's not going to mean anything if we pass amendment four, And then Rick Scott gets reelected, goes back into the Senate, and he wants to be Senate Majority Leader, and then pushes for a national abortion ban. The amendment measure needs 60% of the vote. Every poll that I've seen has us at 62, 63%. That's who we are in Florida. Those are our values. I only need 50 plus one. Tell me I can't win this race in November.",
